<gsak:LonBeforeCorrect>27.00545</gsak:LonBeforeCorrect>
</gsak:wptExtension><groundspeak:cache id="10062257" available="True" archived="False" xmlns:groundspeak="http://www.groundspeak.com/cache/1/0/1">
<groundspeak:name>Sea salt in KOS (Earthcache_Alec2005)</groundspeak:name>
<groundspeak:placed_by>unknown</groundspeak:placed_by>
<groundspeak:owner id="31780268">unknown</groundspeak:owner>
<groundspeak:type>Earthcache</groundspeak:type>
<groundspeak:container>Other</groundspeak:container>
<groundspeak:difficulty>3</groundspeak:difficulty>
<groundspeak:terrain>4.5</groundspeak:terrain>
<groundspeak:short_description html="False"></groundspeak:short_description>
<groundspeak:long_description html="False"></groundspeak:long_description>
<groundspeak:encoded_hints></groundspeak:encoded_hints>
<groundspeak:logs>
<groundspeak:log id="-2">
<groundspeak:type>Write note</groundspeak:type>
<groundspeak:finder id="0">GSAK</groundspeak:finder>
<groundspeak:text>
Vloženo : 2026-09-21
Drive in : ANO
Ověřeno: Na výchozích
Poznámky :
1. 
Monitoring density and salinity (saturation level): Ordinary seawater contains approximately 3.5% salt. For salt to begin crystallizing, the water must evaporate until the concentration rises to roughly 25% (the saturation point, or brine). If fresh seawater were allowed to flow into the pans unchecked, the concentration would constantly drop, resetting the process and preventing the salt from crystallizing at all during the season. 
Multi-stage pan system (fractional crystallization): Production took place within a system of interconnected pools that became progressively smaller and shallower (preparation, evaporation, and crystallization pans). Only a precise amount of water could be admitted to each stage. The water remained in each pool only until it reached a specific density, at which point it was transferred to the next stage.
Separation of unwanted minerals: Seawater contains not only sodium chloride (table salt) but also calcium sulfate (gypsum) and magnesium or potassium salts. By carefully regulating water levels and density in the individual pans, gypsum was made to settle out in the initial evaporation pans, while pure salt—free from the bitter aftertaste of magnesium—formed in the crystallization pans.
Controlling salt crust thickness: If there were too much water in the pans, it would not evaporate completely before the end of summer. If there were too little of it, the layer of deposited salt would be extremely thin, making it difficult to scrape off by hand and collect with wooden rakes without mixing the salt with the mud from the bottom of the pan.

2.
Ideal Mediterranean climate: Tigaki utilized a natural cycle in which a lagoon filled with seawater during the winter. During the hot, dry summer months, intense sun and wind evaporated the water completely—without any human intervention—leaving behind only a pure salt crust.
No fuel consumption: While people in the north (such as in the Baltic or North Sea regions) had to burn vast quantities of wood or peat just to bring rain-diluted water to a boil and &amp;quot;boil out&amp;quot; the salt, fuel consumption for the crystallization process itself in Tigaki was zero.
3.
5 windows.
Nemocnice :
2026-10-06 - Odpovědi a finální souřadnice se týkají keše GC41F9J - Salt lake Alikes in Tigaki.
Tento záznam by se měl vymazat z databáze.
2026-10-05 - Automatická kontrola : U finálky je uvedeno, že se nachází na HC, ale FC jsou jiné. (21.03km)
</groundspeak:text>
</groundspeak:log>
</groundspeak:logs>
</groundspeak:cache>
</wpt>

